					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jakdragonx" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5588260); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5588260&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606833911" alt="JakdragonX" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5588260)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jakdragonx" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5588260); return false;" >JakdragonX</a></span> <span class="odate time_1605144655 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">12 Nov 2020 01:30</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4816594">
									

<p>Although I'm rather distasteful towards the thought that someone &quot;speaking&quot; to their loved one (assumingly out of grief or loss) would directly cause the aforementioned loved one to become temporarily reanimated and feel <em>pain</em> from that interaction, I'm comfortable enough to also give a tentative upvote. The fact of the matter is that this article made me <em>feel</em> uncomfortable, which I didn't necessarily expect.</p>
<p>It's rather simple and to the point; however, the only <em>legitimate</em> critique I have is that I still don't necessarily understand what this anomaly exactly is, or what it's intended to be. I understand that it's meant to be intentionally vague, but I still feel like I have no clue what I'm really reading about. Besides that, however, I did enjoy this.</p>

